Issues.Body:
### .NET version

Reproduced on .NET 10 (Microsoft.WindowsDesktop.App 10.0.15) and .NET 11 (11.0.0). The code involved is the same on `main`.

### Did it work in .NET Framework?

No

### Did it work in any of the earlier releases of .NET Core or .NET 5+?

No. The same repro also fails on .NET Framework 4.8 with `ArgumentException: Item has already been added. Key in dictionary: 'System.Windows.Forms.BindingContext+HashKey'`, so this is long-standing behaviour and not a regression.

### Issue description

`BindingContext.EnsureListManager` looks up the (data source, data member) key in its cache, creates the binding manager, and then calls `_listManagers.Add(key, ...)` without looking the key up again. Creating the manager can run arbitrary code, and that code can call back into the same `BindingContext` for the same key. The nested call registers a manager first, so the outer `Add` throws:

```
System.ArgumentException: An item with the same key has already been added. Key: System.Windows.Forms.BindingContext+HashKey
   at System.Collections.Generic.Dictionary`2.TryInsert(TKey key, TValue value, InsertionBehavior behavior)
   at System.Collections.Generic.Dictionary`2.Add(TKey key, TValue value)
   at System.Windows.Forms.BindingContext.EnsureListManager(Object dataSource, String dataMember)
   at Program.<Main>$(String[] args)
```

WinForms itself opens this window. When the parent list is empty, `RelatedCurrencyManager` calls `AddNew()` and then `CancelCurrentEdit()` on the parent `CurrencyManager` while it is still being constructed. That raises `ListChanged` and `PositionChanged` on the parent before the new manager has been registered. If a handler of those events binds a deeper member of the same chain (in our case the handler shows a panel, which creates a control that is bound to a grandchild list), the nested lookup creates and registers the manager for the child list, and the outer lookup then fails.

A list property getter that triggers binding has the same effect.

**Expected:** the outer lookup returns the manager that is already registered.

**Actual:** `ArgumentException` from `Dictionary.Add`.

A possible fix is to look the key up again after the manager has been created and, if a manager has been registered in the meantime, return that one and discard the new one (unhooking it from its parent manager and its list).

### Steps to reproduce

Console project with `<UseWindowsForms>true</UseWindowsForms>` and implicit usings enabled:

```csharp
using System.ComponentModel;

Root root = new();                                   // Parents is empty
BindingContext context = new();
CurrencyManager parents = (CurrencyManager)context[root, "Parents"];

bool reentered = false;
parents.PositionChanged += (s, e) =>
{
    // Any handler that binds a deeper member of the same chain,
    // for example by showing a control that is bound to it.
    if (!reentered && parents.Count > 0)
    {
        reentered = true;
        _ = context[root, "Parents.Children.GrandChildren"];
    }
};

// Throws System.ArgumentException: An item with the same key has already been added.
_ = context[root, "Parents.Children"];

public class GrandChild { public string? Name { get; set; } }
public class Child { public BindingList<GrandChild> GrandChildren { get; } = new(); }
public class Parent { public BindingList<Child> Children { get; } = new(); }
public class Root { public BindingList<Parent> Parents { get; } = new(); }
```

The lookup succeeds if `Parents` already contains an item, or if the handler does not bind anything.
